ical Drop: 535 m"]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The second men’s Olympic super-G was wide open coming into Albertville. " [:a {:href "/athletes/86562"} "Franz Heinzer"] " had won the 1991 World Cup, while " [:a {:href "/athletes/83156"} "Paul Accola"] " was leading the 1991-92 World Cup race. The 1991 World Champion had been " [:a {:href "/athletes/85391"} "Stephan Eberharter"] ", but he competed only in the combined in Albertville, with the silver medal going to the young Norwegian " [:a {:href "/athletes/99335"} "Kjetil André Aamodt"] ". The course was quite short at 1,650 metres, almost 700 metres shorter than the Calgary super-G and thru 2006, the shortest super-G course by almost 400 metres. Swiss " [:a {:href "/athletes/86433"} "Marco Hangl"] " set the pace as the second skier off, posting 1:13.90. Next up was Aamodt, who moved ahead with 1:13.04. Aamodt was followed by Luxembourgeois " [:a {:href "/athletes/86083"} "Marc Girardelli"] ", likely the greatest skier never to win an Olympic medal to this time. Girardelli could not beat Aamodt, but his time of 1:13.77 would end his unofficial title, getting the silver medal. Accola would finish 10th while Heinzer would fall and not finish."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Time"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "3"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/99335"} "Kjetil André Aamodt"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NOR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NOR.png"}] "NOR"]] [:td {} "1:13.04"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "4"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/86083"} "Marc Girardelli"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/LUX"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-